  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "The quadratic $x^2+mx+n$ has roots twice those of $x^2+px+m=0$, and none of $m$, $n$, and $p$ is zero. Find $\\frac{n}{p}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac{n}{p}=8$.\n\nDoubling the roots doubles their sum and multiplies their product by $4$.",
      "worked": "Let the roots of $x^2+px+m=0$ be $r$ and $s$. By Vieta’s formulas, $r+s=-p$ and $rs=m$. The other quadratic has roots $2r$ and $2s$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}-m&=2(r+s)\\\\-m&=-2p,\\\\m&=2p.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\begin{align*}n&=(2r)(2s)\\\\n&=4m\\\\n&=8p,\\\\\\frac{n}{p}&=8.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 84
  },
